
Parsing Commands.
=================

Aim: 	to produce a "simple" parser which is "smart"
	AND supports the old system (via an inheritable object etc).

	Must be fast and space efficient.


Current System.
---------------

We have 4 individual types of actions -
* add(<func>, "", 1);		Suck ALL input.
* add(<func>, "'", -N); 	Match first N letters (no space necessary)
				[note: only ever used for 1 letter commands]
* add(<func>, "word", N);	Match the first N letters (or more).
* add(<func>, "word");		Match on "word" match.

Actions get added from a variety of objects for varying lengths of time -
* rooms - Usually actions added by rooms are very temporary
	  with players running through many rooms.
* items - these are semi-permanent and tend to remain in a players
	  inventory for a short length of time.
* souls - these are often effective for the entire login time of the
	  player.

Actions are kept in LIFO order (and searched in that order for
a match).

This is an ugly system with the only identified object being
the "actor" (who gives the command); all other parsing is done
by individual object:functions.


Suggested System.
----------------

Replacing add_action().

Unfortunetly we must be able to accommodate most of the current system
within the any suggested system [ unless there is a straight-forward
transformation we could apply ].
Unless we make serious changes some kind of list structure must
be kept to maintain the current in-order searching of
verbs.

[ I'll discuss supporting the current system first ]

To aim for speed we'd ideally maintain a hash table for matches
but this is only realistic for complete word matches.

I suggest the following restrictions: 
	"xverb" matches be restricted to 1 letter and 
	kept in a seperate list for initial matching 
	(these have 'maximum' priority).

	Because input-trappers are generally used to 
	disable commands or perform a task that needs to be always perform:
	removal of non-priority (in-order) trapping of input.
	Input trappers shall be kept in a seperate list
	which shall be checked FIRST. Cases throughout the mudlib
	which do not conform to this will have to be dealt
	with appropriately.

An efficient system to implement these would have a "temporary"
list constructed from rooms generally [ note exits could even
by removed from being added and have the moving object simply 
query the room for an exit - especially since only room.c would
need to be changed ].

A more permanent system for all other actions would be appropriate.
We can use a hash table for full word matches. For partial word
matches we can add every instance possible for a partial match
to the hash table (ie. add(<func>, "inventory", 1) would
add "i", "in", "inv", "inve", "inven", etc..). Although this
is somewhat space inefficient we still maintain our
fast-match capability using the hash_value() efun.
If we use an external hash table we can also still keep the
order of matches. 


This reduces to 
	
array temporary_matches;	/* list of "temporary" matches" 
				   only 1 object has matches here */
array trap_input[MAX_TRAP];	/* list of input trappers */
array xverbs[MAX_XVERB];	/* list of single characters to match */
array word_table;		/* hash table of the rest of the words */

/* all input runs through catch input */
catch_input(string input)
{

	/* call all input trappers - return 1 stops processing */
	/* check temporary array */
	/* check for matches in xverbs */
	/* check hash table */


	/* 
	   If we ever get a "match" (from the checks) then
	   we call the object:function(string) [ if old system ]
	   If it returns 1 we finish
	   else if 0 check for more matches
	   if we end up with a 0 "notify_fail"
	   (default "What ?")
	*/
}

* add_action() could be added as an emulated efun.


Preferred System.
=================

Here's a rundown of what I think the system *should* work like.
I'm not interested in perfect english but analysing intent of a 
sentence. Some example sentences -

* burp, fart, sing, dance, etc..
* smile gandalf, kill fearsome, get sword, etc..
* smile at gandalf, look at harry, look in box, etc..
* get the sword, get a sword, 
* get a sword from the box, get all the swords from the box,
* get all from the corpse, give all flowers to harry,
* get the sword and an apple from the haystack,
* get 4 blue potions and the 2nd pink sword
* get 4 potions and sword 2 
* look under the wall
* look behind dredd and harry
* drop the book, the orange knives, 2 green apples.
* empty the bag on the ground.
* turn the hand on the clock to 5 o'clock

Clearly it is difficult to support all these types of sentences
without substantial overhead. But to be able to break down
sentences and identify objects referred to makes programming
commands *much* easier (and centralised the identification process).

First we must examine how mud commands are structured:
( [ bracketed items are optional ] ).

sentence:
 	command [quantity] [article] [noun-list] 
			[ [ preposition ] [ noun-list ] ] 

conjunction: "and"

command: imperative ( most mud verbs )
	| query ( "who", "what", "where" )

quantity: "all" | word-number | number

article: "the" | "a" 

noun-list: [ adjective ] noun
	| [ adjective ] noun "," noun-list
	| noun-list conjunction [ adjective ] noun 


We want to identify the following -
actor - (easy) the object which gives the command
primary participants - objects which the actions occur upon
secondary participants - varied role (bags etc)

With extra information about commands (when they're being added)
we can also identify incomplete sentences and prompt the user
for completion or clarification [ ala Zork style ] without having 
to re-type the entire sentence.

When we've identified primary participants etc these
can be passed directly to the object:function performing the
action (relieving it of the job of string parsing).

In addition to this I believe all actions should
have a "time" associated with them and players should be
able to "command-ahead" with their commands being queued
while they wait for their actions to be performed.

This system is more difficult to implement.

Integrating this system with support for the old system
will require some nasty hack-work.

Anyway - I think the first part of this (supporting the old
system) should be fairly straightforward to implement
and will see about doing it soon.

Feedback Welcome.

Dredd

